engine: model:blind-entailer
output: |
  body: |
    The statement: for every natural number k, if k is not 0, then the set of natural numbers p such that p is prime and p is congruent to 1 modulo k is infinite. Here p is congruent to 1 modulo k when p and 1 leave the same remainder on division by k; modulo 1 every number is congruent to 1. [C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one, L:term/congruent-modulo-k]

    The logic runs one way: build, for each bound n, one such prime above n, then restate that as an infinite set. The file's text holds no `sorry` and declares no axiom of its own, but it was read here as text and nothing was built or run, so whether Lean accepts it is not known here. [C:part/one-beyond-every-bound, P:claim/checking, P:assumption/read-as-text]

    Stage 1, the reduction, says it is enough to produce, for each bound n, one such prime p with n < p. Its key lemma, Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one, says: for every natural number k and every natural number n, if k is not 0, there is a prime p with n < p and p congruent to 1 modulo k. To build p, the file evaluates the k-th cyclotomic polynomial: the monic integer polynomial whose complex roots are exactly the primitive k-th roots of unity. [C:part/one-beyond-every-bound, C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one, K:concept/cyclotomic-polynomial]

    Stage 2 handles the modulus one. Since k is not 0, either k = 1 or k > 1. For k = 1 every number is congruent to 1, so any prime at least n + 1, from the infinitude of primes, serves. It is split off because the bound used next is stated for k > 1. [C:part/the-modulus-one, C:step/case-k-one, C:reliance/infinite-primes, N:decision/split-off-k-one]

    Stage 3 builds the candidate prime. For k > 1 put b = k times n factorial, where n factorial is the product 1 times 2 times ... times n; then b is at least 2. The absolute value of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial at b exceeds b - 1, so it exceeds 1 and has a least prime factor p, the smallest divisor greater than 1, which is always prime. Since p divides that value, the image of b in the integers modulo p (the field of remainders on division by p) is a root of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial there. One reading: the proof can name a prime at all only because the value is greater than 1. [C:part/the-candidate-prime, L:term/n-factorial, L:term/least-prime-factor, L:term/the-integers-modulo-p, C:reliance/cyclotomic-value-bound, C:reliance/cyclotomic-reduction, P:reading/the-value-has-a-prime-factor]

    Stage 4 puts the candidate past the bound. A root of a cyclotomic polynomial modulo p is coprime to p, so p does not divide b. Every number from 1 to n divides n factorial and so divides b; a prime p at most n would divide b, so n < p. One reading: the factor n factorial is what makes the prime large. [C:part/past-the-bound, C:reliance/root-coprime, C:reliance/divides-factorial, P:reading/factorial-pushes-past-n]

    Stage 5 shows the order of b is exactly k. The multiplicative order of an element is the least positive power of it that equals 1. Since b is nonzero modulo p, its order divides p - 1, by the little theorem of Fermat. Since k divides b and p does not, k is nonzero modulo p, so the root b is a primitive k-th root of unity (its k-th power is 1 and no smaller positive power is) and its order is exactly k. Hence k divides p - 1, so p is congruent to 1 modulo k. One reading: the congruence comes from the order, not from any search, and the factor k in b is what keeps k nonzero modulo p. [C:part/order-exactly-k, K:concept/multiplicative-order, K:concept/little-theorem-of-fermat, K:concept/primitive-root-of-unity, C:reliance/cyclotomic-root-primitive, P:reading/order-forces-the-congruence, P:reading/the-factor-k-keeps-k-nonzero]

    Stage 6 returns to infinitely many. Its key lemma, Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one, says: for every natural number k, if k is not 0, then frequently as p goes to infinity, p is prime and congruent to 1 modulo k. A property holds frequently at infinity when it holds for arbitrarily large p. The strict n < p is weakened to n at most p, and on the natural numbers holding frequently at infinity is the same as holding on an infinite set. One reading: this is only a change of language. [C:part/back-to-infinitely-many, C:lemma/Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one, L:term/frequently-at-infinity, C:reliance/frequently-infinite, P:reading/only-a-change-of-language]

    A worked example: take k = 3 and n = 2, so b = 3 times 2 factorial = 6. The third cyclotomic polynomial is x squared plus x plus 1, whose value at 6 is 43. Since 43 is prime, p = 43; 43 > 2, and 43 - 1 = 42 = 3 times 14, so 43 is congruent to 1 modulo 3. Also 6 cubed = 216 = 5 times 43 plus 1, so 6 has order exactly 3 modulo 43. [P:case/three-and-two]

    What the proof delivers is more than the statement keeps. For each k other than 0 and each bound n it names one prime congruent to 1 modulo k above n. For k > 1 the argument uses only that p is a prime dividing the value, so every prime factor of the absolute value of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial at k times n factorial is above n and congruent to 1 modulo k; the least one lies between n, exclusive, and that absolute value, inclusive. The statement keeps only that the set of such primes is infinite. [P:yield/what-the-proof-delivers, N:decision/least-prime-factor]
